VIEWING IT
═══════════════════════════════════════════

6. Driver dashboard
    http://<CURRENT_DROPLET_IP>:8501

7. Mechanic dashboard (service console — emergency/maintenance queues,
   ATDT diagnose/repair chat, OBD source toggle)
    http://<CURRENT_DROPLET_IP>:8501/mechanic

Both are served by the same backend, same process.

═══════════════════════════════════════════
TESTING END-TO-END (after any rebuild or major change)
═══════════════════════════════════════════

8. Data source / sensors sanity check

    curl localhost:8501/api/mechanic/obd-mode
    curl localhost:8501/api/sensors
    curl localhost:8501/api/data-source

If override is mqtt/adapter but active_source shows "none", nothing is
currently publishing — either the ESP32 isn't running, or (for testing
without the car) start the fake publisher:

    python3 mqtt_fake_publisher.py
    # or in the background, surviving disconnects:
    nohup python3 mqtt_fake_publisher.py > fake_publisher.log 2>&1 &

9. ATDT diagnose/guidance

    curl -X POST localhost:8501/api/atdt/diagnose \
      -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"query": "rough idle"}'

    curl -X POST localhost:8501/api/atdt/guidance \
      -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"query": "how do I check the brake pads"}'

Known symptom keys diagnose recognizes: high_o2_correlation, rough_idle,
engine_overheating, o2_sensor_failure, engine_misfire, lean_fuel_mixture,
rich_fuel_mixture, transmission_slip, cat_efficiency_below_threshold, etc.
Vague queries get asked for more detail rather than guessing — that's
correct behavior, not a bug.

10. Manual mechanic-push test (doesn't wait for a real fault)

    curl http://localhost:8501/api/mechanic/push-test

11. Confirm it landed

    curl -s -u ditto:ditto http://localhost:8090/api/2/things/org.example:MECHANIC_001
    curl -s http://localhost:8501/api/mechanic/status
    curl -s http://localhost:8501/api/mechanic/emergency | python3 -m json.tool

Note: alert dedup is active (autonomous/action_engine.py). The mechanic
twin is only re-pushed on a severity change, or after a cooldown if the
same severity persists — don't expect a new push on every tick during a
sustained fault. Look for
"[ACTION ENGINE] duplicate within cooldown, skipping mechanic push"
in the logs to confirm it's working as intended, not silently broken.

12. "MECHANIC LINK ONLINE/OFFLINE" on the console ≠ vehicle data status.
    It only reflects whether the mechanic's own Ditto instance is
    reachable (service_layer/mechanic_client.py's is_mechanic_connected()).
    A car can be sending perfectly good live telemetry while this shows
    OFFLINE if the mechanic Ditto container is down, and vice versa.
    Use /api/data-source (the "LIVE (MQTT)" chip) to check actual vehicle
    data flow instead.

═══════════════════════════════════════════
SHUTTING DOWN / DESTROYING TO SAVE COST
═══════════════════════════════════════════

13. Stop the backend

    systemctl stop acdt.service

14. Stop the Docker stack

    docker compose down

Your data (InfluxDB, Neo4j, Mongo, both Ditto Mongo instances) persists in
volumes as long as the droplet itself isn't destroyed — stopping
containers alone loses nothing.

15. BEFORE destroying the droplet entirely — checklist (learned the hard
    way once already):

    a) git status  → must say "nothing to commit, working tree clean"
       (check --ignored too, to catch any untracked-but-important files
       like a new test script that never got added)

    b) cat .env  → copy this output somewhere OUTSIDE the droplet
       (password manager, encrypted note, etc.) — this is the exact file
       that gets lost on destroy and needs to be fully retyped otherwise

    c) Note current droplet specs for the rebuild (currently:
       4 vCPU / 8GB RAM, NYC1 — update this line if you resize/change it)

    Only destroy once a) and b) are both done.

16. Resizing the droplet down instead of destroying (cheaper than a full
    rebuild if you'll need it again soon)

Fully supported by DigitalOcean, not a hack. To do it safely:

  a) Power off the droplet (required before any resize):

     ssh root@<CURRENT_DROPLET_IP> "shutdown -h now"

     Wait ~30-60s, then confirm it's off in the DO control panel.

  b) Resize — DO control panel:
     Droplet page → "Resize" → pick a smaller plan → make sure "Only
     increase disk size to match new plan" is UNCHECKED (this is the
     CPU/RAM-only option) → Resize. If checked, the disk resize is baked
     in permanently even if you resize down again later.

     Or via doctl CLI:

     doctl compute droplet-action resize <droplet-id> \
       --size <smaller-slug> --resize-disk=false

     doctl compute droplet list   # to find <droplet-id> and current slug

  c) Power back on (DO usually does this automatically after a resize):

     ssh root@<CURRENT_DROPLET_IP>
     # if this fails: power on from the DO panel, then retry SSH

  d) Resize back up before your next session — same steps, larger plan,
     disk checkbox still unchecked.

  e) Once back up: resume from PRODUCTION step 1 (SSH in,
     docker compose up -d, confirm containers, systemctl status).

